{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2061 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "730d6084b039d6a905b7f87954dec77a7135509d902f52468209b7c852107c11" :edb/db-before-t 2060 :edb/db-after-t 2061 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535373 50 #inst "2026-10-08T23:02:21.430Z" 13194139535373 true] [17592186184096 1020 #edb/ref 17592186346437 13194139535373 true] [17592186184096 1020 #edb/ref 17592186346444 13194139535373 true] [17592186184096 1020 #edb/ref 17592186346451 13194139535373 true] [17592186184542 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535373 true] [17592186184542 1100 "Consider the function $f(x) = x^{2} - 2e^{x}$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $f(x)$ is concave up on $(- ∞, 0)$\n2. $f(x)$ is concave down on $(- ∞, 0)$\n3. $f(x)$ is concave down on $(0, ∞)$" 13194139535373 false] [17592186184542 1100 "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{2}-2{e}^{x}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-\\infty ,0)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(-\\infty ,0)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(0,\\infty )$" 13194139535373 true] [17592186184542 1145 "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =2x-2{e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =2-2{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-2{e}^{x} & >0 \\\\ {e}^{x} & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this inequality by considering the graph of $y={e}^{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063674/assets/c199b110a3d19ecc23454f52dc7470af318ce871805595fd5fe7609dee5b0545.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that ${e}^{x}<1$ when $x<0.$ So the graph is concave up on the interval $(-\\infty ,0).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality ${e}^{x}>1,$ which has the solution $(0,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139535373 true] [17592186184547 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535373 true] [17592186184547 1100 "Consider the function $f(x) = e^{x}\\sin x$, defined on $x ∈ (0, π)$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $f(x)$ is concave down on $(0, \\frac{π}{2})$\n2. $f(x)$ is concave up on $(\\frac{π}{2}, π)$\n3. $f(x)$ is concave down on $(\\frac{π}{2}, π)$" 13194139535373 false] [17592186184547 1100 "Consider the function $f(x)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ defined on $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi )$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi )$" 13194139535373 true] [17592186184547 1145 "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x={e}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & >0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this inequality by considering the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ over the interval $(0,\\pi ).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063674/assets/410e0af2ed20a065c27da5edaf0095dfd22c8a37a92e3d8fbaeece629cefa278.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0$ when $0<x<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ So the graph is concave up on the interval $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ which has the solution $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139535373 true] [17592186346437 1017 #edb/ref 17592186346438 13194139535373 true] [17592186346437 1089 "q-114061" 13194139535373 true] [17592186346437 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535373 true] [17592186346437 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063674/assets/d3bd98f5662140655dd2bd33f99dc8704b1bd03b6004133dc8492ac51ea3a347.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y={h}^{″}(x).$  Find the intervals on which the function $h(x)$ is concave down." 13194139535373 true] [17592186346437 1113 #uuid "8d160f05-f6c3-5cab-b428-a1db0a9e077b" 13194139535373 true] [17592186346437 1145 "A function $h(x)$ is concave down over an interval $(a,b)$ if ${h}^{″}(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nOn the given graph of $y={h}^{″}(x),$ we can see that ${h}^{″}(x)<0$ on the intervals $(-4,-2)$ and $(2,4).$  Therefore, the function $h(x)$ is concave down on the intervals $(-4,-2)$ and $(2,4).$" 13194139535373 true] [17592186346438 1000 #edb/ref 17592186346439 13194139535373 true] [17592186346438 1000 #edb/ref 17592186346440 13194139535373 true] [17592186346438 1000 #edb/ref 17592186346441 13194139535373 true] [17592186346438 1000 #edb/ref 17592186346442 13194139535373 true] [17592186346438 1000 #edb/ref 17592186346443 13194139535373 true] [17592186346438 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535373 true] [17592186346438 1041 "selection" 13194139535373 true] [17592186346438 1104 #edb/ref 17592186346440 13194139535373 true] [17592186346438 1110 #uuid "c2c4c27b-802b-576d-a604-f7f8dd87a004" 13194139535373 true] [17592186346439 1034 "(-\\infty ,-4)" 13194139535373 true] [17592186346439 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535373 true] [17592186346439 1111 #uuid "2253a318-f02f-50db-96d3-30c172a6e1bb" 13194139535373 true] [17592186346440 1034 "$(-4,-2)$ and $(2,4)$" 13